Description: survive configuration reloads under concurrent lookups
 Every entry point re-reads the configuration file when it changes, and
 reset_config() freed each server's key and cleared its address while
 another thread was inside lookup_tacacs_user() using them: a commit of a
 TACACS+ server change could crash configd, which resolves users from many
 goroutines. 0003's mutex covered only the parse. Parsing now takes a
 read-write lock for writing, and every entry point that reads the
 configuration runs under it for reading (its own get_config() call
 returns the result parsed just before). test/config_threads (make
 check, built with AddressSanitizer) runs lookups against a listener while
 the file is rewritten; it reported a heap-use-after-free of a key (strlen)
 before this change. Lookups still write tac_srv[].not_resp under the read
 lock: a plain int flag, as before. The test does not check leaks:
 reset_config() never frees a server's getaddrinfo() result (upstream).
Author: NuDanOS Maintainers <jon@fernandez.tech>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-18
---
